course: dictionary
lesson: 12027
title: Chomping: noisy eating and eagerness
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Chomping» is a lively word for eating. When you chomp, you chew noisily and move your jaw in big movements. Read the words around it to check: «The horse was chomping on sweet hay in its stable» tells you the horse is eating, and the noise is part of the picture.
explain: Chomping can also show eagerness. «Lily was chomping at the bit before the race» does not mean Lily was eating anything. The phrase means she was very eager and impatient to start. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: The phrase comes from horses. A horse holds a metal bit in its mouth, and when it is keen to run, it chews and tugs at the bit. Think of that picture when you read «chomping at the bit», and you will remember the feeling of impatience.
fact: Chomping means chewing or biting noisily, with big movements of the jaw.
fact: «Chomping» is the -ing form of the verb «chomp». Other forms are chomps, chomped and chomp.
fact: «Chomping at the bit» means very eager and impatient to start something.
fact: The phrase comes from a horse biting on the metal bit in its mouth.
fact: Chomping can describe eating or eagerness, so check the context to see which meaning the author intends.
exercise: «The horse was chomping on sweet hay in its stable.» What is the horse doing? | *Chewing noisily with big jaw movements | Sleeping quietly in the sun | Drinking water from a bucket | Running fast across the field
exercise: «Lily was chomping at the bit before the race.» What does chomping at the bit mean? | Tired after a long run | *Very eager and impatient to start | Bored and ready to go home | Angry at the race organisers
exercise: «Tom chomped an apple so loudly that Mum laughed.» Find and copy the word that shows noisy eating. | laughed | *chomped | Mum | apple
test: «The dog chomped on its bone happily.» What does chomped mean? | Hid behind the sofa | Stared at the door | *Chewed noisily | Buried under the garden
test: Why might an author write that a runner was chomping at the bit? | To show the runner was cold and wet | To show the runner was sleepy and slow | *To show the runner was eager and impatient to start | To show the runner had already lost
exam: Which phrase shows someone is excited and impatient before a game? | Quiet as a mouse | Feeling sick after lunch | Fast asleep on the sofa | *Chomping at the bit
